id: k_mutex_lock
title: Protect shared data with a mutex
category: Mutexes
version: 4.4.0
aliases: lock mutex|acquire lock|protect shared variable|critical section|prevent data race|mutex wait|synchronise shared data
signature: int k_mutex_lock(struct k_mutex *mutex, k_timeout_t timeout);
source: https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/v4.4.0/include/zephyr/kernel.h#L3527
related: K_MUTEX_DEFINE|k_mutex_init|k_mutex_unlock|k_sem_take
## Explanation
Give one thread exclusive access to shared state until it calls k_mutex_unlock(). Unlike a semaphore, a Zephyr mutex has an owner and supports priority inheritance.
## Parameters
mutex: pointer to an initialized Zephyr k_mutex.
timeout: K_NO_WAIT to try once, K_FOREVER to block, or a finite duration.
Returns: 0 on successful lock, -EBUSY for a no-wait failure, or -EAGAIN after a timeout.
## Example
#include <zephyr/kernel.h>

K_MUTEX_DEFINE(counter_lock);
static int counter;

static void worker(void *a, void *b, void *c)
{
    ARG_UNUSED(a);
    ARG_UNUSED(b);
    ARG_UNUSED(c);
    while (1) {
        if (k_mutex_lock(&counter_lock, K_FOREVER) == 0) {
            counter++;
            k_mutex_unlock(&counter_lock);
        }
        k_sleep(K_MSEC(100));
    }
}

K_THREAD_DEFINE(worker_id, 1024, worker, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 0);

int main(void)
{
    while (1) {
        if (k_mutex_lock(&counter_lock, K_FOREVER) == 0) {
            int snapshot = counter;
            k_mutex_unlock(&counter_lock);
            printk("Counter: %d\n", snapshot);
        }
        k_sleep(K_SECONDS(1));
    }
    return 0;
}
## Requirements
Assumptions: regular supervisor-mode Zephyr application with kernel threads; extra RAM for the worker stack. No Devicetree pin, peripheral or additional Kconfig option is needed for this example beyond normal threading support.
For printed output, enable CONFIG_PRINTK and a suitable console in the project/board configuration if they are not already enabled.
## Mistakes
Mutexes cannot be locked or unlocked in ISR context. Always unlock in the same thread that acquired the mutex and handle timeout/failure before using the resource. Avoid doing slow operations while holding a mutex. Nested locking by the same owner is permitted, but requires matching unlock calls; watch for lock-order deadlocks.
